mptcp-bpf-bkup (1.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Use a backup subflow only when no other subflow is active, as the
    default scheduler does. A subflow was skipped only when both backup flags
    were set, which they rarely are: a router WAN flagged as backup carried
    traffic like the others, and once no other subflow was left the transfer
    stopped instead of moving to the backup one. A non-backup subflow whose
    send buffer is full is waited for rather than replaced by a backup one.
  * Add a get_retrans making the default scheduler's choice and call
    mptcp_set_timeout() in get_send: the oldest data was sent again every
    200 ms on the subflow already holding it.

 -- Yannick Chabanois <contact@openmptcprouter.com>  Fri, 09 Oct 2026 13:14:19 +0000

